<item>
		<title>NASA Names Stephen Cash Marshall Space Flight Center Director</title>
		<link>https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-names-stephen-cash-marshall-space-flight-center-director/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Gerelle Q. Dodson]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:14:39 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Leadership]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Marshall Space Flight Center]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[NASA Headquarters]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.nasa.gov/?post_type=press-release&#038;p=1057238</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman has named Stephen Cash director of the agency’s Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama, effective Monday, Oct. 19. Cash, a veteran NASA engineer and aerospace executive, returns to lead the center where he began his career more than four decades ago. “Marshall contributions to NASA&#8217;s missions are historic and it&#8217;s [&#8230;]]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<div id="" class="hds-media hds-module wp-block-image"><div class="margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline"><div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit "><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1638" height="2048" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?w=1638" class="attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048" alt="Stephen Cash" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="eager" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg 2400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=240,300 240w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=768,960 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=819,1024 819w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=1229,1536 1229w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=1638,2048 1638w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=320,400 320w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=480,600 480w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=720,900 720w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=960,1200 960w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/steve-cash-photo.jpg?resize=1600,2000 1600w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1638px) 100vw, 1638px" /></a></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Photo of Stephen Cash </div></figcaption></div></div></div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman has named Stephen Cash director of the agency’s Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama, effective Monday, Oct. 19. Cash, a veteran NASA engineer and aerospace executive, returns to lead the center where he began his career more than four decades ago.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Marshall contributions to NASA&#8217;s missions are historic and it&#8217;s no question why it&#8217;s the Center of Excellence for propulsion,” said Isaacman. “Steve Cash is a Marshall legend, has spent his career around the hardware, the people and has been in the seat before making the decisions when human spaceflight is on the line. As Marshall continues its critical role in Artemis and takes on new challenges in advanced propulsion, nuclear power, and the systems that will carry us deeper into the solar system, Steve brings the experience, technical judgment, and mission focus needed to lead the center into that next chapter. I am also grateful to Rae Ann Meyer for the leadership she has provided to Marshall during this transition and for her continued service to NASA.”&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Leading a NASA center requires a commitment to its people and to the technical capabilities entrusted to them,” said Amit Kshatriya, NASA associate administrator and chief engineer. “Steve understands that the jewel of Marshall is its workforce and that engineering excellence is sustained by giving talented people the opportunity to design, build, test, and learn through hands-on work. As Marshall continues strengthening the manufacturing, structural loads and dynamics, and propulsion capabilities that underpin NASA’s exploration enterprise, Steve’s leadership will help ensure the workforce has opportunities to develop and apply the expertise needed to deliver the agency’s most ambitious missions.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"> As center director, Cash will lead one of NASA’s largest field centers, overseeing a workforce of more than 5,000 civil service and contractor employees and a broad portfolio of propulsion, space systems, scientific research, and technology development activities.  Marshall plays a critical role in NASA’s Artemis program, including the development of NASA’s SLS (Space Launch System) rocket, management of the Human Landing System Program, as well as other capabilities supporting sustained exploration of the Moon and future missions to Mars. Further, Marshall plays a key role in the advancement of nuclear power and propulsion, an area of focus that is expected to grow as NASA works towards sending the first humans to Mars.  </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“It is a profound honor to be back home at the Marshall Space Flight Center,” said Cash. “I am humbled to stand alongside the brilliant minds here as we continue to turn the impossible into everyday history.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Cash began his NASA career at Marshall in 1982 and spent 34 years with the agency, holding senior engineering and management positions supporting some of the nation’s most significant human spaceflight programs.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">During his NASA career, Cash served as manager of Marshall’s Space Shuttle Propulsion Office, overseeing critical propulsion systems supporting shuttle through its final missions. He later served as director of Marshall’s Safety and Mission Assurance Directorate from 2011 until his retirement in 2016, providing leadership in safety, reliability, and quality engineering to support mission success across the center.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Following his retirement from NASA, Cash continued his work in the aerospace industry, including serving as vice president for advanced space systems at Axient, supporting the development and execution of major spaceflight programs. In 2024, he was inducted into the Alabama Engineering Hall of Fame in recognition of his contributions to engineering and the nation&#8217;s space program.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Cash succeeds Rae Ann Meyer, who has served as Marshall’s acting center director since September 2025. Meyer, who previously served as Marshall’s deputy center director and associate director, has provided leadership and continuity across the center’s programs and operations during her tenure as acting director. Meyer will return to her role as Deputy Center Director.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Established in 1960, Marshall has played a central role in American space exploration for more than six decades, from developing the Saturn rockets that carried astronauts to the Moon during Apollo to advancing the propulsion systems, infrastructure, and technologies supporting the next era of human spaceflight.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For more information about NASA’s Marshall Space Flight Center, visit:</p>



<p class="has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/marshall"><strong>https://www.nasa.gov/marshall</strong></a></p>



<p class="has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph">-end-</p>



