					<description><![CDATA[Shortly after NASA&#8217;s Artemis II mission splashed down on April 10, 2026, U.S. Navy divers captured underwater imagery of the Orion spacecraft’s heat shield. In the months after, the agency measured whether the thermal protection system effectively safeguarded the crew and Orion systems as the spacecraft reentered Earth’s atmosphere. This rigorous analysis confirmed that the [&#8230;]]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<div id="" class="hds-media hds-module wp-block-image"><div class="margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline"><div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-none "><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg"><img decoding="async" width="2048" height="1365" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?w=2048" class="attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048" alt="The Orion spacecraft&#039;s heat shield dominates this underwater photo. Due to the water, the heat shield appears to be teal-colored." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg 6000w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=768,512 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=1024,683 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=1536,1024 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=2048,1365 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=400,267 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=600,400 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=900,600 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=1200,800 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ksc-20260411-navy01-0045orig-rotated.jpg?resize=2000,1333 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px" /></a></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-credits">US Navy</div></figcaption></div></div></div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Shortly after NASA&#8217;s Artemis II mission splashed down on April 10, 2026, U.S. Navy divers captured underwater imagery of the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/reference/orion-spacecraft/#hds-sidebar-nav-3">Orion spacecraft’s</a> heat shield. In the months after, the agency measured whether the thermal protection system effectively safeguarded the crew and Orion systems as the spacecraft reentered Earth’s atmosphere. This rigorous analysis confirmed that the heat shield performed as expected.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/artemis/artemis-2/rigorous-analysis-confirms-nasas-artemis-ii-heat-shield-performance/?utm_source=TWITTER&amp;utm_medium=NASAArtemis&amp;utm_campaign=NASASocial&amp;linkId=1021788731">Learn how Orion&#8217;s heat shield was examined.</a></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>Image credit: US Navy</em></p>
